---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/marketing.css';
import {getComparison} from '../../data/comparisons';

const c = getComparison('devin')!;

const title = 'Self-Hosted Devin Alternative for Kubernetes | GratefulAgents';
const description =
  'GratefulAgents vs Devin: self-host a coding agent in your Kubernetes cluster with your model contracts and no code leaving your network.';

const faqs = c.faqs;

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/devin/#article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/devin/',
    author: {'@type': 'Organization', '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
    publisher: {'@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/devin/#breadcrumb',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Alternatives',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 3,
        name: 'Devin',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/devin/',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/devin/#faq',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.question,
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: f.answer,
      },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base {title} {description} type="article" {structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="mk-page">

      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol class="mk-breadcrumb">
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          <li><a href="/alternatives/">Alternatives</a></li>
          <li><span aria-current="page">Devin</span></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="mk-layout-2col">
        <div>

          <div class="mk-hero">
            <div class="mk-vs-badge">
              <span class="mk-vs-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              Comparison
            </div>
            <h1>GratefulAgents vs Devin</h1>
            <p>
              Devin is Cognition's fully managed, cloud-hosted autonomous coding agent—one of the
              most polished products in the space. GratefulAgents is a self-hosted, open-source
              alternative that runs the same class of long-horizon coding tasks inside your own
              Kubernetes cluster. This page compares them honestly, including where Devin is the
              better choice.
            </p>
          </div>

          <article class="mk-prose">

            <h2>What Devin is</h2>
            <p>
              Devin is an autonomous AI software engineer built by Cognition AI. It was designed
              from the outset to tackle long-horizon engineering tasks: planning multi-step
              implementations, writing and running code in a sandboxed environment, debugging
              failures, and iterating until a goal is complete. Devin operates as a fully managed
              cloud service—Cognition handles all infrastructure and the product is accessible via
              its web application.
            </p>
            <p>
              Devin's approach is to give the agent a human-level interface: a terminal, a browser,
              and an editor, all within Cognition's managed sandbox. You describe the task, the
              agent plans and executes, and you can observe and steer via the session UI. It
              integrates with GitHub and other code surfaces to read context and push results.
            </p>
            <p>
              Devin is a focused, vertical product: it does one thing—autonomous software
              engineering—and Cognition has invested heavily in doing it well. It is not trying to
              be a general-purpose Kubernetes operator or an extensible trigger platform.
            </p>

            <h2>What Devin is genuinely good at</h2>
            <p>
              {c.whatItIsGoodAt}
            </p>
            <p>
              If your team's primary constraint is not data-residency or IP-protection but rather
              task-completion quality and ease of use, Devin deserves serious evaluation on its own
              merits. For teams without Kubernetes expertise or without a strong reason to
              self-host, Devin's zero-ops model is a genuine and meaningful advantage.
            </p>

            <h2>What changes when you need self-hosted execution</h2>
            <p>
              Devin's cloud-managed architecture has implications that matter for certain
              organisations:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>Your source code executes on Cognition's infrastructure.</strong> During an
                agent session, Devin clones or checks out your code and runs it in Cognition's
                managed sandbox environment. For organisations with data-residency obligations,
                financial services compliance requirements, or contractual IP-protection clauses,
                this may make the product unusable regardless of its quality.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Model choice is Cognition's decision.</strong> Devin runs on Cognition's
                internal models. You do not choose the underlying model, bring your own API keys,
                or apply your negotiated volume contracts. If your organisation has standardised on
                a specific model family or signed an enterprise agreement with a model provider,
                you cannot apply that agreement to Devin's inference costs.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Private infrastructure is unreachable.</strong> Devin cannot reach private
                npm registries, internal Maven repositories, corporate API gateways, or any network
                resource behind a firewall. This limits the kinds of real-world codebases it can
                work with effectively.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>The trace lives in Devin's UI.</strong> Cognition provides a session
                replay and observation interface. If you need a full distributed trace exported to
                your own Jaeger or observability stack—with per-step cost, token counts, and tool
                call details—that is not something Devin's architecture can provide, because the
                execution does not happen in your environment.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Again: none of these are deficiencies in Devin's design; they are the natural
              consequences of a fully managed, cloud-first product. For teams without those
              constraints, they are invisible. For teams with them, they are blockers.
            </p>

            <h2>Dimension-by-dimension comparison</h2>
            <p>
              The following table compares GratefulAgents and Devin on stable architectural
              dimensions rather than current feature lists, which change as both products evolve.
            </p>

            <div class="mk-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Comparison table: GratefulAgents vs Devin">
              <table class="mk-compare-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Dimension</th>
                    <th scope="col">Devin</th>
                    <th scope="col">GratefulAgents</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {c.rows.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{row.dimension}</td>
                      <td>{row.competitor}</td>
                      <td>{row.gratefulagents}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h2>Who should pick which</h2>

            <h3>Choose Devin if:</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>You want a polished, managed autonomous agent that requires no infrastructure setup.</li>
              <li>Your team has no data-residency constraints and is comfortable with code executing on Cognition's infrastructure.</li>
              <li>Task-completion quality and product polish are your primary evaluation criteria.</li>
              <li>You do not have specific model requirements or existing API contracts to apply.</li>
              <li>Your team has limited Kubernetes expertise and you do not want to acquire it.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Choose GratefulAgents if:</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Your organisation has data-residency, regulatory, or IP-protection requirements that prevent source code from running on third-party infrastructure.</li>
              <li>You need your agent to access private registries, internal APIs, or network-restricted resources during a run.</li>
              <li>You want to bring your own model keys—Claude, OpenAI, OpenRouter, Grok, or Copilot—and control inference costs directly.</li>
              <li>You need full audit-grade observability: Jaeger-compatible distributed traces for every run, including step-by-step tool calls, model prompts, costs, and token counts.</li>
              <li>You want a declarative, Kubernetes-native approach to agent scheduling, trigger integrations, and credential management.</li>
              <li>You want mobile clients (iOS, Android) for oversight and steering from outside the office.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="mk-callout mk-callout-neutral">
              <strong>Note on current status:</strong> GratefulAgents is in early development.
              Bugs are expected. There is no documented high-availability configuration, managed
              hosting, or production support SLA. If Devin's polish and managed reliability
              matter to you, those are real advantages at this stage. Evaluate GratefulAgents
              with that context—try it in a non-production environment first.
            </div>

            <h2>Observability in depth</h2>
            <p>
              One area where the self-hosted model provides a structural advantage is observability.
              Because every GratefulAgents run executes in your cluster, the full execution trace
              can be emitted to a Jaeger instance you own. The Agent Ops console surfaces this as:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Per-step trace spans with wall-clock timing</li>
              <li>Each tool call with its inputs and outputs</li>
              <li>Every model prompt and response, with token counts</li>
              <li>Per-run cost aggregated from your model API keys</li>
              <li>Subagent dependency graphs showing how parallel agents coordinated</li>
              <li>Compaction events and errors</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              This level of traceability matters for engineering organisations that need to
              understand why an agent made a particular change, audit agent behaviour for
              compliance purposes, or optimise model selection and prompt strategy over time.
            </p>

            <h2>How to try GratefulAgents</h2>
            <p>
              The recommended evaluation path is a local Kind cluster—no cloud account required,
              just Docker and about fifteen minutes. The
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/">Kind getting-started guide</a>
              walks through the full setup: bootstrapping the cluster, deploying the Helm chart
              (including PostgreSQL, MinIO, and Jaeger), adding model credentials, connecting a
              GitHub repository, and running your first agent task.
            </p>
            <p>
              For a server-based installation, the
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/">k3s guide</a> covers deploying
              on a fresh Debian or Ubuntu server.
            </p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
                Get started with Kind →
              </a>
              <a href="/alternatives/" class="btn btn-quiet">More comparisons</a>
            </div>

            <hr class="mk-divider" />

            <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
            <div class="mk-faq">
              {faqs.map((f) => (
                <details class="mk-faq-item">
                  <summary>{f.question}</summary>
                  <div class="mk-faq-answer">{f.answer}</div>
                </details>
              ))}
            </div>

          </article>

          <div class="mk-disclaimer">
            <p>
              Devin® is a trademark of Cognition AI, Inc. All trademarks are the property of their
              respective owners. This comparison reflects the authors' understanding of each product
              at time of writing; product capabilities change frequently. Consult
              <a href="https://devin.ai" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cognition's own Devin documentation</a>
              for authoritative and up-to-date information.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <aside>
          <div class="mk-sidebar-card">
            <h3>Try GratefulAgents</h3>
            <p>Self-hosted. Open source. Runs in your Kubernetes cluster.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
                Kind quick-start →
              </a>
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/" class="btn btn-quiet">
                k3s on a server
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>More comparisons</h3>
            <p>See how GratefulAgents compares to other coding agent options.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/alternatives/github-copilot/" class="btn btn-quiet">vs Copilot →</a>
              <a href="/alternatives/openhands/" class="btn btn-quiet">vs OpenHands →</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>Why self-host?</h3>
            <p>Architecture guide covering data residency, IP protection, and cost control.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/self-hosted-coding-agents/" class="btn btn-quiet">Read guide →</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
